  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nSouthwest low level flow will continue today ahead of a weak cold \nfront dropping down from the northwest. This will lead to the \ncontinuation of well above normal temperatures, with afternoon highs\nin the mid to upper 70s. \n\nMoisture is limited with the cold front tonight, but some of the \nhigh res models are trying to produce isolated-scattered light rain \nshowers in an axis of weak instability along and ahead the front \nacross our northwest this evening. Any shower activity will then \nlikely dissipate as the front pushes southeast overnight and we lose\nthe diurnal instability. \n\nWeak low level CAA behind the front will lead to a slight cool down \nwith highs on Friday ranging from the upper 60s north to the lower \n70s in the south and highs on Saturday in the 65 to 70 degree range.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nThere remains some uncertainty with the strength of mid level energy\nmoving into the Ohio Valley through the second half of the weekend \nand how much moisture will accompany it. The ECMWF and Canadian are \npretty weak/dry with the system while the GFS and the tail end of \nthe NAM are trying to bring pcpn in from the southwest through the \nday on Sunday. As a result, will keep some chances for pcpn Sunday \ninto Sunday night, but confidence is not very high.\n\nModels continue to show a more dynamic system approaching from the \nwest through mid next week, but there remains a fair amount of spread\non the timing and strength of this system. As a result, will \ncontinue to broad brush chance pops through much of the extended \nperiod, with temperatures remaining several degrees above normal \nthrough mid week.",
